<section id="learn" class="section active">
<div class="heading"><span class="tag">LEARN</span><h2>Energy–Wave Vector (E–K) Diagram</h2>
<p>The Energy–Wave Vector (E–K) diagram is a graphical representation showing the relationship between the energy <b>E</b> of an electron and its wave vector <b>K</b> in a crystalline solid. It is obtained from the Kronig–Penney model and is used to explain allowed energy bands, forbidden energy gaps and Brillouin zones.</p></div>

<div class="card"><h3>Wave Vector (K)</h3>
<p>The wave vector represents the wave nature of an electron moving inside a crystal and is given by</p>
<div class="eq">K = 2π/λ</div>
<p>where <b>K</b> is the wave vector and <b>λ</b> is the wavelength associated with the electron.</p>
<div class="key"><b>Remember:</b> The wave vector is directly related to the momentum of the electron through <b>p = ħK</b>.</div>
</div>

<div class="card"><h3>Energy of a Free Electron</h3>
<p>For a free electron, the kinetic energy is</p>
<div class="eq">E = p²/2m</div>
<p>Using the relation <b>p = ħK</b>,</p>
<div class="eq">E = ħ²K² / 2m</div>
<p>This equation gives a parabolic E–K curve, indicating that the energy of a free electron increases continuously with wave vector.</p></div>

<div class="card"><h3>Energy Bands in a Crystal</h3>
<p>When an electron moves inside a crystal, it experiences the periodic potential produced by the regularly arranged atoms. Because of this periodic potential, the continuous free-electron energy curve is modified and separates into <b>allowed energy bands</b>.</p>
<p>These allowed bands are separated by <b>forbidden energy gaps</b>. No permitted electron energy level exists inside a forbidden energy gap.</p>
<div class="key"><b>Origin of the band structure:</b> Periodic crystal potential → modified E–K relation → allowed energy bands + forbidden energy gaps.</div></div>

<div class="card"><h3>Brillouin Zones</h3>
<p>The wave-vector axis is divided into regions called <b>Brillouin zones</b>. Each zone represents a range of wave vectors in the reciprocal lattice.</p>
<div class="grid">
<div class="mini"><strong>First Brillouin Zone</strong><p>−π/a ≤ K ≤ π/a</p></div>
<div class="mini"><strong>Second Brillouin Zone</strong><p>π/a ≤ |K| ≤ 2π/a</p></div>
<div class="mini"><strong>Third Brillouin Zone</strong><p>2π/a ≤ |K| ≤ 3π/a</p></div>
</div>
<p>Here <b>a</b> is the lattice constant of the one-dimensional crystal.</p></div>

<div class="card"><h3>Key Features</h3>
<ul>
<li>Energy varies continuously within an allowed energy band.</li>
<li>Forbidden energy gaps appear at the boundaries of Brillouin zones.</li>
<li>The energy bands are symmetrical about <b>K = 0</b> for the one-dimensional representation.</li>
<li>The first Brillouin zone is especially important because it contains the unique information needed to describe electron motion in the periodic crystal.</li>
<li>Electrons can exist only in allowed energy bands.</li>
</ul></div>
</section>
<section id="free" class="section"><div class="heading"><span class="tag">FREE ELECTRON</span><h2>Free-Electron E–k Relation</h2></div><div class="card"><p>For a free electron,</p><div class="eq">E = p²/2m</div><p>Using p = ħk,</p><div class="eq">E = ħ²k²/2m</div><p>Hence the E–k relation is parabolic.</p></div><div class="card figure"><svg viewBox="0 0 900 500"><rect width="900" height="500" fill="white"/><g font-family="Arial"><text x="450" y="35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="24" font-weight="bold" fill="#173f7a">Free Electron: E = ħ²k²/2m</text><line x1="110" y1="430" x2="110" y2="70" stroke="#555" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="110" y1="430" x2="820" y2="430" stroke="#555" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M170 110 Q465 430 760 110" fill="none" stroke="#2868a9" stroke-width="6"/><text x="80" y="90">E</text><text x="825" y="450">k</text><text x="465" y="475" text-anchor="middle" fill="#687887">Parabolic dispersion relation</text></g></svg><p>Fig. 1: Free-electron E–k relation.</p></div></section>
<section id="crystal" class="section">
<div class="heading"><span class="tag">CRYSTAL</span><h2>E–K Diagram for a Crystal</h2>
<p>The E–K diagram obtained from the Kronig–Penney model shows how the periodic crystal potential produces alternate allowed energy bands and forbidden energy gaps.</p></div>

<div class="card">
<h3>Allowed and Forbidden Regions</h3>
<table style="width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13px">
<tr style="background:#f2f7fb;color:#173f7a"><th style="padding:13px;border:1px solid #dfe7ee;text-align:left">Region</th><th style="padding:13px;border:1px solid #dfe7ee;text-align:left">Meaning</th></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:13px;border:1px solid #dfe7ee"><b>Allowed Energy Band</b></td><td style="padding:13px;border:1px solid #dfe7ee">Electrons can possess energy within this range.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:13px;border:1px solid #dfe7ee"><b>Forbidden Energy Gap</b></td><td style="padding:13px;border:1px solid #dfe7ee">No permitted electron energy levels exist in this region.</td></tr>
</table></div>

<div class="card">
<h3>Reference E–K Diagram</h3>
<div class="figure">
<img src="data:image/png;base64," alt="E-K diagram showing first Brillouin zone, allowed bands and forbidden gaps" style="display:block;width:100%;max-width:900px;height:auto;margin:0 auto;border-radius:6px;">
<p>Fig. 2: E–K diagram showing the first Brillouin zone, allowed energy bands and forbidden energy gaps.</p>
</div>
<p>The central region from <b>−π/a to +π/a</b> is the <b>first Brillouin zone</b>. The next intervals represent the second and third Brillouin zones. The shaded gap regions indicate forbidden energies, while the curved portions between them represent allowed energy bands.</p>
<div class="key"><b>Reading the figure:</b> At the boundaries of the Brillouin zones, the periodic lattice produces gaps in the energy spectrum. Thus the electron cannot possess arbitrary energy values inside the crystal.</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="interactive" class="section"><div class="heading"><span class="tag">INTERACTIVE</span><h2>Explore the E–k Curve</h2><p>Increase the periodic potential and observe stronger band splitting near the zone boundary.</p></div><div class="card figure"><svg id="svg" viewBox="0 0 900 500"><rect width="900" height="500" fill="white"/><g font-family="Arial"><text x="450" y="35" text-anchor="middle" font-size="22" font-weight="bold" fill="#173f7a">Interactive E–k Diagram</text><line x1="100" y1="430" x2="100" y2="70" stroke="#555" stroke-width="2"/><line x1="100" y1="430" x2="820" y2="430" stroke="#555" stroke-width="2"/><text x="75" y="85">E</text><text x="825" y="450">k</text><g id="curves"></g></g></svg></div><div class="card"><label style="font-size:12px">Periodic potential: <b id="strength">Medium</b></label><input id="slider" type="range" min="0" max="4" value="2" style="width:100%"><div class="key" id="info"></div></div></section>
<section id="meaning" class="section">
<div class="heading"><span class="tag">SIGNIFICANCE</span><h2>Physical Significance of the E–K Diagram</h2>
<p>The E–K diagram provides a visual description of the electronic band structure of a crystalline solid.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Importance of Brillouin Zones</h3>
<ul>
<li>They simplify the study of electron motion in crystals.</li>
<li>They help explain the origin of electronic band structure.</li>
<li>They help distinguish conductors, semiconductors and insulators.</li>
<li>They are widely used in solid-state physics and semiconductor engineering.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Physical Significance</h3>
<ul>
<li>The E–K diagram clearly shows the formation of allowed and forbidden energy bands.</li>
<li>It explains why electrons in crystals do not possess arbitrary continuous energy values.</li>
<li>It forms the basis for understanding electrical conductivity.</li>
<li>The width of the band gap helps determine whether a material behaves as a conductor, semiconductor or insulator.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Connection with Effective Mass</h3>
<p>The slope and curvature of the E–K curve provide additional information about electron motion.</p>
<div class="eq small">v<sub>g</sub> = (1/ħ)(dE/dK)</div>
<div class="eq small">m* = ħ² / (d²E/dK²)</div>
<p>This provides the natural connection to the next topic: <b>Effective Mass of an Electron</b>.</p></div>
<div class="card"><h3>Important Formulae</h3>
<div class="eq small">K = 2π/λ</div>
<div class="eq small">E = ħ²K² / 2m</div>
<div class="eq small">First Brillouin Zone: −π/a ≤ K ≤ π/a</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="practice" class="section"><div class="heading"><span class="tag">PRACTICE</span><h2>Quick Check</h2></div>
<div class="question"><p>1. E–k means:</p><button class="option" onclick="ans(this,true,'q1')">A. Energy versus wave vector</button><button class="option" onclick="ans(this,false,'q1')">B. Energy versus temperature only</button><div class="answer" id="q1">Correct: A.</div></div>
<div class="question"><p>2. Free-electron E–k relation is:</p><button class="option" onclick="ans(this,true,'q2')">A. E = ħ²k²/2m</button><button class="option" onclick="ans(this,false,'q2')">B. E = ħk</button><div class="answer" id="q2">Correct: A.</div></div>
<div class="question"><p>3. A periodic potential can produce:</p><button class="option" onclick="ans(this,true,'q3')">A. Allowed bands and forbidden gaps</button><button class="option" onclick="ans(this,false,'q3')">B. No energy states</button><div class="answer" id="q3">Correct: A.</div></div>
<div class="question"><p>4. Group velocity is related to:</p><button class="option" onclick="ans(this,true,'q4')">A. dE/dk</button><button class="option" onclick="ans(this,false,'q4')">B. dE/dt only</button><div class="answer" id="q4">Correct: A.</div></div>
<div class="question"><p>5. E–k curvature is related to:</p><button class="option" onclick="ans(this,true,'q5')">A. Effective mass</button><button class="option" onclick="ans(this,false,'q5')">B. Atomic volume only</button><div class="answer" id="q5">Correct: A.</div></div></section>
<section id="revision" class="section"><div class="heading"><span class="tag">QUICK REVISION</span><h2>Remember These Points</h2></div><div class="revision"><div><strong>Definition</strong><p>E–k is energy as a function of wave vector.</p></div><div><strong>Free electron</strong><p>E = ħ²k²/2m, a parabola.</p></div><div><strong>Crystal</strong><p>Periodic potential modifies E(k).</p></div><div><strong>Bandgap</strong><p>Forbidden gaps separate allowed bands.</p></div><div><strong>Velocity</strong><p>v<sub>g</sub> = (1/ħ)dE/dk.</p></div><div><strong>Effective mass</strong><p>m* depends on E–k curvature.</p></div></div><div class="card"><div class="key"><b>Exam focus:</b> The E–k diagram represents allowed electron energies as a function of wave vector. A free electron has a parabolic relation; a periodic crystal potential produces bands and forbidden gaps.</div></div></section>
